  "text": "_MetadataUsageId\n\nTo solve the problem, we need to find the area of the quadrilateral \\(BCGF\\) in a regular hexagon \\(ABCDEF\\) inscribed in a circle with center \\(O\\). Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Understanding the Hexagon and Circle:**\n   - A regular hexagon has 6 equal sides and 6 equal angles.\n   - The radius of the circle is the same as the side length of the hexagon because in a regular hexagon, the radius is the same as the side length.\n   - The center \\(O\\) of the circle is also the center of the hexagon.\n\n2. **Midpoints:**\n   - \\(G\\) is the midpoint of arc \\(BC\\).\n   - \\(H\\) is the midpoint of side \\(AC\\).\n\n3. **Segments \\(AG\\) and \\(DE\\):**\n   - Extend \\(AG\\) and \\(DE\\) to meet at point \\(M\\).\n   - Given \\(AM = 3\\) and the radius \\(OE = 2\\).\n\n4. **Finding the Area of Quadrilateral \\(BCGF\\):**\n   - Since \\(G\\) is the midpoint of arc \\(BC\\), \\(BCGF\\) is a rectangle because \\(G\\) is the midpoint of arc \\(BC\\) and \\(BC\\) is a side of the hexagon.\n   - The height of the rectangle \\(BCGF\\) is equal to the radius of the circle, which is 2.\n   - The length of the rectangle \\(BCGF\\) is equal to the arc length of \\(BC\\), which is half the length of the side of the hexagon. Since the radius is 2, the side length of the hexagon is also 2.\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle \\(BCGF\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\text{height} \\times \\text{width} = 2 \\times 2 = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of quadrilateral \\(BCGF\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
